  "dom_id": "step-q267390",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/8feb78ada73af4c97e7ce60555cf22acf5e24da38e30424c4c5c430e6456c06d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/41c6badfbcafe7bafdddca30606511d2d6c3c8bdbbcd5ec2e29ff9c4c2dd310f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\n$$",
